it is a journey through remote towns and  villages to get close enough to see the  power plants which is now under russian  military control  from territory still held by ukrainian  forces major valentin yermalenko  points out where the fighting has been  most intense  the closeness of this war to these  nuclear reactors has caused warnings of  disaster and international condemnation  on the horizon steam hangs over the  plant a sign that it is still  functioning  but the haze over the buildings makes it  difficult to see the damage where the  russian shelling started fires  the nuclear power plant is just over  there across the dinipro river it's  about four miles from our position here  now this is close as we can get safely  we've been brought here by a military  escort  we've been asked to keep low because  there is the danger  of incoming fire now there is still  fighting going on to the right of the  plant  and if anything was to happen to it  well it would add a really worrying  dimension to this conflict it would have  implications not just for ukraine  but for all of europe  these pictures show flares lighting up  the plants as the russians launched  their assault  the cctv network recorded as the tanks  rolled in  grainy pictures but it's clear just how  dangerous this situation could become  foreign  russia claims it's only firing at  military targets but this school in  sotomir tells another story  so many families are being displaced by  this war  these were residential buildings in the  city of chernhiv  but look at them now  as the emergency services try to put out  the flames  residents don't know where to turn  [Music]  and as the russian invasion moves  further into the country more and more  people are fleeing  this queue at a checkpoint just outside  dunipro goes on for miles and miles  many more families will be forced to run  before this conflict is over  alex rossi sky news  zaparizia
 

